  "query_preview": "..\nThis afternoon through Saturday: An upper level low pressure system \nwill track from eastern Oregon today into eastern Idaho by Saturday. \nMoisture pushing north ahead of this low will bring up moisture with \nup to a 50% chance of showers across the southeast portion of the \nforecast area through this evening. Mid level lapse rates also \nsteepen with an isolated thunderstorm threat with these showers as \nwell. Best instability will generally be over the higher terrain of \nthe Blue Mountains in northeast Oregon to the Clearwater Mountains \nin the southern Idaho Panhandle. Storms will then be moving into an \nenvironment that won't be as conducive for continuing to support \nthunderstorm development as the push further north into places like \nthe the Palouse to the Silver Valley. Decaying convection will still \nhave the potential to produce at least occasional cloud to ground \nlightning strikes gusty outflow winds up 30 mph, and brief heavy \ndownpours. Latest convective allowing model guidance has weakened \nthe potential outflow boundary produced by the convection this \nevening. It's not completely out of the question that blowing dust \nwill occur with the winds, but chances look much less likely \ncompared to what these models were indicating this time yesterday. \nShowers are expected to continue into the evening, but the \nconvective potential will be decreasing as we lose the surface \nheating. As such, I expect shower coverage to be less over the \nsoutheast portion of the forecast area, and the potential for \nthunderstorms will rapidly decrease as well.\n\nWe won't be as unstable on Friday and Saturday as the low makes its \nmarch eastward across Idaho. The fringes of the southeast portion of \nour forecast area will continue to see the potential for diurnally \nforced convection in near the Camas Prairie and in the St. Joe \nForest in the Central Panhandle Mountains. Otherwise, it looks like \nwe won't be unstable enough for convection to initiate off of the \nhigher terrain further north and west, e.g. over the Palouse. \nTemperatures will remain warm and above average into the weekend. \nCloud cover also will be on the decrease as the upper level pulls \naway.\n\nSaturday night through Thursday: Good agreement among the model \nensembles with a ridging of high pressure building in over the \nregion into early next week. This will keep our temperatures warm \nwith highs in the upper 70s to low 80s. Models also are generally in \ngood agreement with the overall longwave pattern with troughing of \nlower pressure in the eastern Pacific and ridging of higher pressure \nover the Rockies. Where models disagree and uncertainty is \nintroduced is how close in proximity the trough will be to the \nNorthwest region. The general consensus is that temperatures \nwill be cooler starting Tuesday into Wednesday, but how much \ncooler is still very much up in the air. Right now it is 50/50 \nif we see cooler than average temperatures with an increased \nchance for showers, particularly over the Cascades, or continue \nwith a dry forecast with temperatures at or slightly above \nnormal for Thursday into Friday next week. /SVH",
